# Skills heal themselves or they rot

A skill encodes how the world worked on the day it was written. Boards redesign
forms, hosts stop resolving, APIs move. Without a standing instruction to write
fixes back, every session pays full price to rediscover the same workaround, and
the file quietly becomes a liability that is followed and wrong.

**Every skill carries a `## Self-healing and self-improvement` section.** Copy
the one in `src/skills/core/job-application/SKILL.md`. It says: when a step fails
during use, fix the immediate problem, establish what changed with evidence, then
edit the failing section in the same session. Replace the stale instruction
rather than appending a second version beside it — two contradictory
instructions are worse than one stale one. When the task succeeds and something
was learned, add the trap and delete the step that has never mattered.

## Ship sequence

```bash
git pull --ff-only origin main   # other sessions commit here concurrently
pnpm run skills:compile          # `pnpm run check` does NOT recompile
pnpm run check                   # must exit 0; read the exit code, not the tail
git add src/skills/core/<name>/SKILL.md
git commit && git push origin main
pnpm run skills:link
```

Edit `src/skills/core/<name>/SKILL.md` only. A compiled copy under `dist/` or a
linked copy under `~/.claude/skills/` is generated output; an edit there is lost
on the next build and never reaches the other machine.

Verify the linked file itself rather than the link summary. Linking after a
`check` but without a `skills:compile` publishes the previous build, and the
summary still reports success.

## Gates a new skill must pass

- The name must exist in `src/skills/skill-rules.map.json`, or `skills:compile`
  fails with "missing in skill-rules.map.json".
- The description needs a `Trigger when` clause starting before character 150,
  so it survives listing truncation, and a `Do not use` exclusion boundary.
- It needs an action verb from `scripts/constants/ACTION_WORDS.ts` (`audits`,
  `fixes`, `plans`, `reviews`, `migrates`...). "Handles" and "manages" fail.
- `gitleaks` runs on pre-push and matches prose. A phrase like "access codes,"
  in a table trips `generic-api-key`. Reword the sentence; do not add an
  allowlist entry for a false positive, and do not bypass the hook.

## Skills do not live in project repositories

`.claude/` is gitignored in most project repos, so a skill written there exists
on one disk and does not survive a clone. That is how a job-search skill sat
unversioned from July to September 2026. Author it in rocket-agents even when
its subject matter is personal and project-specific; `invoice-quarter-close`,
`favish-weekly-review` and `job-application` all are.
